437	\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}
438	\label{sec:apply-ldt}
439	
440	The combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next
441	merged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that
442	encode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is
443	done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking
444	the quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test
445	(Theorem~\ref{lem:ld-soundness}). The classical game, its question
446	distribution, and the line-point distribution are defined at dimension $2m+2$
447	only when $2m+2$ divides $q$ (Definition~\ref{def:ld-game}), a condition that
448	admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ does not supply; the statements below are the
449	source statements, without that condition, and their proofs are blocked at
450	the instantiation step for admissible parameters with $m \ge 2$. The
451	obstruction is recorded in Remark~\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility} and
452	analyzed, together with candidate repairs, in
453	\cite{gap:qpbt_ld-dimension-divisibility}.
